86414080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414080 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $2x+5y=200?$\n\n- $(20,32)$\n- $(10,20)$\n- $(15,35)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34033db1-99ab-5af1-b893-026e522543dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414080 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(20,32)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=20$ and $y=32,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 20+5\\cdot 32 & =200 \\\\ 40+160 & =200 \\\\ 200 & =200\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(10,20)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=10$ and $y=20,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 10+5\\cdot 20 & =200 \\\\ 20+100 & =200 \\\\ 120 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(15,35)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=15$ and $y=35,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 15+5\\cdot 35 & =200 \\\\ 30+175 & =200 \\\\ 205 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $5x+9y=8.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "385bffa1-c86b-563c-a6fa-f591b8055eb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414081 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=5,$ $b=9,$ and $c=8.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(5,9)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9 & = & 5\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 5 & = & 4\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =9-5\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1 & =5-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5-4 \\\\  & =5-(9-5) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1) & =1 \\\\ 5\\cdot 2\\cdot 8+9\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 8 & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot 16+9\\cdot (-8) & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(16,-8)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(16+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-8-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(16+9t,\\,-8-5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=16+9t$ is attained when $t=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=16+9\\cdot (-1)=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $5x+6y=8?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-8,8)$\n- $(4,-4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689613bd-55bf-5236-b19b-6e6b928e5d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414082 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 2 & =8 \\\\ -10+12 & =8 \\\\ 2 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(-8,8)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=-8$ and $y=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-8)+6\\cdot 8 & =8 \\\\ -40+48 & =8 \\\\ 8 & =8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-4) & =8 \\\\ 20-24 & =8 \\\\ -4 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $6x-4y=10,$ which of the following gives the general solution to the equation? \n\n*Hint: Assume that $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77383f6b-915f-5d79-b4cc-4a0aa033b4bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414083 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$ Moreover, if $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n({x}_{0}+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,{y}_{0}-\\frac{a}{d}t),\\,t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=6,$ $b=-4,$ $c=10,$ and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(6,-4)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $(1,-1)$ is a solution of $6x-4y=10,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\frac{(-4)}{2}t,\\,-1-\\frac{6}{2}t) & =(1-2t,\\,-1-3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\n- $15x-20y=5$\n- $11x+9y=13$\n- $-8x+2y=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8955bc35-57b9-5c55-b8c0-47deb511cca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414084 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=15,$ $b=-20,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(15,-20)=5\n$$\n\nand $5\\,|\\,5,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has solutions. We have $a=11,$ $b=9,$ and $c=13.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,9)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,13,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=-8,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-8,2)=2\n$$\n\nand $2\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,5,$ our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $3x+6y=-9,$ and that $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x,$ what is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c669e825-e611-5b7c-b04d-1fdff3741695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414085 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  Moreover, if $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n({x}_{0}+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,{y}_{0}-\\frac{a}{d}t),\\,t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=3,$ $b=6,$ $c=-9,$ and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,6)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-2)$ is a solution of $3x+6y=-9,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{6}{3}t,\\,-2-\\frac{3}{3}t)=(1+2t,\\,-2-t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=1+2t$ is attained when $t=0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=1+2\\cdot 0=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\n- $3x+7y=11$\n- $-3x-9y=2$\n- $-12x+15y=21$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccf1e2c5-efff-5b3d-ba7c-30f178649720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414086 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=3,$ $b=7,$ and $c=11.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,11,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=-3,$ $b=-9,$ and $c=2.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-3,-9)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,2,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-12,$ $b=15,$ and $c=21.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-12,15)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,|\\,21,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
